<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kavad I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">473–<a href="/years/531">531</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kavad I was Sasanian King of Kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> from 488 to 496 and again from 498 or 499 to 531. He owed his throne to Hephthalite support and, after nobles deposed him for favouring the Mazdakite movement, regained it with a Hephthalite army. His later reign saw wars with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> and the suppression of the Mazdakites, and he was succeeded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-i">Khosrow I</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kavad-i.jpg" alt="Kavad I" width="320" height="317" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: None · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plate_with_king_hunting_rams_(white_background).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 488 a Hephthalite army beat the Sasanian army of Balash and enthroned <b>Kavad I</b>, and in 498 the king gave Kavad 30,000 troops to regain his throne in return for Chaganiyan; Joshua the Stylite records Kavad leading Hephthalite troops against the Romans at Theodosiupolis in 501–502 and…</blockquote><a href="/empire/hephthalite-empire">Hephthalite Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 520s King Gurgenes rose against <b>Kavad I</b> and appealed to the Byzantine Emperor Justin I, but when Persian armies invaded he fled with his family and nobles to Lazica, according to Procopius.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-iberia">Kingdom of Iberia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…most significant administrative reform of the period was made by Khosrow I, building on initiatives of his father <b>Kavad I</b>. He replaced the traditional harvest-share tax, which required annual assessment and invited official corruption, with a fixed land tax based on surveys of cultivated area, crop type and…</blockquote><a href="/empire/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Sasanian Empire Mazdak called for an egalitarian society and the abolition of monarchy and was soon executed by <b>Kavad I</b>.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/anarchism">Anarchism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Khosrow I, known as Anushirvan, succeeded his father <b>Kavad I</b> as Sasanian King of Kings and went on to negotiate an end to the Iberian War with the Byzantine Empire.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-13">13 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Under al-Harith ibn Amr in the early 6th century, Kinda reached its peak and even held al-Hira for a time at the expense of the Lakhmid Kingdom, reportedly installed there by the Sasanian king <b>Kavad I</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kinda">Kingdom of Kinda</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesiphon">Ctesiphon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.09,44.58&amp;year=510" aria-label="Ctesiphon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5691 962 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Kavad I"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6141 1232h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6141 1232h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33.09,44.58&amp;year=510">What was here in 510 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
